1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,300
Section twelve, the capstone.

2
00:00:02,580 --> 00:00:10,857
We make the project explain itself, ask it questions, answer a real question about congestion pricing, and declare who uses our work.

3
00:00:11,957 --> 00:00:14,218
Documentation in a wiki goes stale.

4
00:00:14,498 --> 00:00:24,461
In dbt, it lives in the same YAML files as the tests, in the same change as the code, and dbt turns it into a website with a lineage graph.

5
00:00:25,561 --> 00:00:27,908
The documentation page, version one.

6
00:00:28,188 --> 00:00:29,745
Descriptions go in YAML.

7
00:00:30,025 --> 00:00:42,100
Text needed in many places goes in a doc block, written once and used everywhere. dbt docs generate builds the documentation, and dbt docs serve opens it as a website.

8
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:46,976
A doc block in a Markdown file explains payment types once.

9
00:00:47,256 --> 00:00:50,818
Every YAML file that has a payment type column points to it.

10
00:00:51,098 --> 00:00:53,850
One text, four columns, always consistent.

11
00:00:54,950 --> 00:01:05,041
In the demo, the agent creates doc blocks for payment type, rate code and trip validity, uses them everywhere, and makes sure every model and column has a description.

12
00:01:05,321 --> 00:01:08,649
Tedious work across many files, perfect for an agent.

13
00:01:09,749 --> 00:01:12,010
Then we generate the docs and open the site.

14
00:01:12,290 --> 00:01:18,349
Every model, column, test and query is there, with a lineage graph that matches the one in Rosetta.

15
00:01:19,449 --> 00:01:21,433
Now the project explains itself.

16
00:01:21,713 --> 00:01:28,006
In Ask mode, we ask the agent to trace the congestion fee from the source to every model that uses it.

17
00:01:28,286 --> 00:01:30,377
It reads the files and gives the path.

18
00:01:31,477 --> 00:01:39,242
Another good question before any join: what is the grain of the daily summary, and can we join it to trips without multiplying rows?

19
00:01:39,522 --> 00:01:43,426
One row per zone per day, so joining it to trips repeats rows.

20
00:01:43,706 --> 00:01:45,114
The agent should warn us.

21
00:01:46,214 --> 00:01:49,500
And now the question the whole project was building towards.

22
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:54,772
January twenty twenty-five was the first month of congestion pricing in New York.

23
00:01:55,052 --> 00:01:56,993
How much fee was collected, and where?

24
00:01:58,093 --> 00:02:01,421
We ask it in Rosetta's analytics, on top of our marts.

25
00:02:01,701 --> 00:02:05,349
Only valid trips count, and we see boroughs instead of IDs.

26
00:02:05,629 --> 00:02:08,018
Everything we built is inside this answer.

27
00:02:09,118 --> 00:02:17,417
Here is the answer on screen: the total fee collected in January, the share of valid trips that paid it, and the borough that collected the most.

28
00:02:17,697 --> 00:02:20,577
From raw files to a tested, documented answer.

29
00:02:21,677 --> 00:02:23,597
The last block: exposures.

30
00:02:23,877 --> 00:02:29,786
An exposure tells dbt that something downstream depends on our models, like this analysis.

31
00:02:30,066 --> 00:02:34,738
If someone changes the fact table tomorrow, they see the analysis in the lineage.

32
00:02:35,838 --> 00:02:42,025
The agent declares it: a name, a label, the type analysis, an owner, and the two marts it depends on.

33
00:02:43,125 --> 00:02:48,224
And here is the complete project, every node built, with our analysis on the far right.

34
00:02:48,504 --> 00:02:50,317
From two Parquet files to this.
